what do you think of when you first hear  the word mutant do you immediately  imagine cool supernatural powers sure  these powers might make people meet in  some comic books but did you know that  in our world we are all mutants so what  do we mean when we say the word mutant  and what is a mutation to answer this  question we need to go back to the  beginning of human life the union of egg  and sperm cells since both egg and sperm  cells contain DNA we inherit half of our  own DNA from each parent all of the DNA  in a single person's body is called  their genome and to find out how unique  we really are we have to compare our  genomes to our parents genomes DNA is  basically a set of instructions that our  cells use to build proteins which act  like the cogs and wheels with them  cell's proteins influence the way our  bodies appear function and respond to  our environment the DNA instructions for  building proteins are spelled out using  four molecules called nucleotides a C T  and G for short these nucleotides are  the key to mutations to be exact a  mutation is a change in nucleotides and  it automatically translates to a change  in the protein product think of it like  a blueprint when you change the  instructions the final design will  change accordingly most mutations occur  during cell division when a mother cell  fully duplicates its DNA so that each of  its daughter cells can receive a copy  occasionally it mistakes can occur and  one copy of DNA that gets passed down  might be slightly different from the  others the DNA that contains the error  is called the mutated DNA DNA mutations  can occur in two different ways somatic  mutations occur in a person cells during  their lifetime and do not get passed  down to future offspring for example  smoking can cause somatic mutations  which lead to cancer and lung diseases  hereditary mutations on the other hand  are locked into your genes from the  moment you're born that's because they  occur in a sperm or egg cell of your  parent before or shortly after they fuse  into an embryo  the first cell that makes you hereditary  mutations are transmitted to you and  your children because your embryo  becomes the template for all of your  cell development when a hereditary  mutation happens for the first time in  your embryo it's called de novo which  means new because it didn't exist in  your parents  a recent study estimates that every one  of us is born with about sixty de novo  mutations on the other hand you may  inherit old hereditary mutations from  your ancestors both old and new  hereditary mutations will be passed down  to future generations each adding to the  genetic variation within a population  genetic variation consists of the unique  family traits or characteristics of a  certain population within a certain  geographical region and it's the reason  we're not exactly the same as our  relatives there is no one else in this  whole world who has the exact same  genome as yours all thanks to mutations  most hereditary mutations are neutral as  far as we can tell they have no  observable impact on health or outer  appearance mutations are an inherent  part of life so why do we need them if  mutations did not occur we wouldn't have  any new genetic variation after a while  everyone would be made up of a singular  dominant genome this would be fine as  long as our environment stayed the same  but our environment is fickle and nature  selects which random mutations helped us  survive as the world changes around us  this is how evolution works and it  depends on the genetic variation  provided by mutation without genetic  variation we wouldn't be able to adapt  to environmental changes so our  mutations don't necessarily give us cool  super powers but now you know they give  us the power to survive  you
 

